    "body": "`nvim --gui` runs Nvim with a GUI in the same process: Qt on a thread of its own, attached like a remote UI (ext_linegrid, ext_multigrid, ext_messages, ext_cmdline, ext_popupmenu) through a plain C bridge (src/nvim/gui). Built with -D ENABLE_GUI=ON.\n\nbiscide shows a Go or Rust program as cards on a canvas: the active declaration with its file's parts, what uses it, what it uses, the one before it. Each card is a window on the file's real buffer, kept to its part. Its runtime is runtime/lua/biscide; its manual is :help biscide.\n\nThe GUI draws its own file tree, messages panel, status line, pickers and popup menu. Other text files open as a single card.\n\nNew API:\n- nvim_gui_set_keymap(), nvim_gui_get_keymap(), nvim_gui_del_keymap() and vim.keymap.set(..., { gui = true }): a mapping layer looked up before buffer-local and global mappings.\n- nvim_win_set_range(), nvim_win_get_range(), nvim_win_del_range(): a window kept to a range of its buffer's lines; the cursor and operators stay in it, and it follows the edits.\n\nAI-assisted\n\n<!--\n  Thank you for contributing to Neovim!\n  If this is your first time, check out https://github.com/neovim/neovim/blob/master/CONTRIBUTING.md#pull-requests-prs\n  for our PR guidelines.\n-->\n\n## Problem\n\n## Solution\n",
